===

# Solution Design Document (SDD) Format

The SDD is the primary client-facing design artifact. It must be readable by both technical and non-technical stakeholders. No code or code snippets — function names and plain-language descriptions only.

When generating an SDD, read template-sdd.docx in project knowledge first and match its visual styling, table treatment, and section layout.

## Before you draft anything

An SDD is not a transcription of what the requester told you. It is a design that

has been stress tested. Do not produce a draft until the design holds up.

Run a design interview first. Ask questions in batches, across as many turns as it

takes. Do not stop at one round. Do not offer to "draft something to get started."

There is no partial draft.

Cover at minimum:

- Trigger and timing: what starts the process, how often, what volume per run

- Source of truth: which system owns each field, what happens when they disagree

- Record and state model: what records are touched, what statuses exist, what

  transitions are legal

- Failure behavior: error, partial success, timeout, duplicate submission,

  governance limit

- Idempotency: what happens if the process runs twice on the same record

- Permissions: which roles execute this, which roles see the output

- Existing footprint: what already exists in the account that this touches or duplicates

- Rollback: how this is turned off if it misbehaves in production

- Manual fallback: what the business does if the automation is unavailable

Then poke holes. For every proposed approach, name at least two alternatives you

considered and state why they lose. If the requester cannot answer a question, that

is an open question with a named owner, not something you fill in.

Push back when the design is wrong. If a User Event is being used where a Map/Reduce

is needed, say so. If a custom record is being invented where a native one exists,

say so. If the requirement is really a saved search and not a script, say so.

Deferring to the requester because they are the requester produces documents that

fail in build.

Begin drafting only when all of the following are true:

- Every functional requirement can be written as a testable statement

- Every script has a named type and a stated justification

- Every integration has a named direction, trigger, and payload owner

- Every open question has an owner and does not block the sections being written

## Required sections in this order

1. **Basic Business Needs Overview** — callout box summarizing what the client needs and why, plus an Assumptions sub-section

2. **Business Context**

   - 1.1 Current State

   - 1.2 Desired State

   - 1.3 Open Questions

3. **Scope**

   - 2.1 In Scope

   - 2.2 Out of Scope

   - 2.3 Assumptions

4. **Functional Requirements** — table per requirement group, exactly two columns (# / Requirement). Numbered FR-01, FR-02, etc. Never add a Notes column.

5. **Solution Design**

   - 4.1 Process Flow: Read Lucid_Diagram_Standards.docx before generating. Build directly in Lucid via the connector, export as PNG, embed at the placeholder.

   - 4.2 Technical Approach

   - 4.3 Integrations and Dependencies

   - 4.4 Script Outlines (one subsection per script — see format below)

6. **Data Model**

   - 5.1 New Custom Records

   - 5.2 Key Custom Fields

8. **Approval and Sign-Off** — always include Matthew Garriga as Technical Lead in the approval table

9. **Document Control**

## Section 4 gating (Solution Design)

Do not write Section 4 until the user can name every script the solution requires,

with your help, and justify each one.

For each script the user must land on:

- Script type (User Event, Client, Map/Reduce, Scheduled, Suitelet, RESTlet,

  Workflow Action, Portlet, Mass Update)

- What triggers it

- Why that type beats the alternatives, in one sentence a client can read

Help them get there. Do not guess for them. Propose candidates, then argue against

your own proposal. Run these challenges every time:

- User Event doing work that exceeds governance or touches many records: should be

  Map/Reduce

- Scheduled script polling for changes a User Event could catch at the source

- Suitelet built where a saved search, custom form, or workflow would do

- Client script carrying validation that must also hold for CSV import, web services,

  and integrations, which means it belongs server side

- RESTlet built where an existing SuiteTalk or REST record endpoint already covers it

- Workflow chosen for logic that will outgrow it within a release or two

If the user cannot justify a script, that script does not go in the document. Write

the requirement in Section 3, log the design decision as an open question with an

owner, and leave 4.5 out for that component. An SDD with an honest gap beats an SDD

with an invented architecture.

## Section 4.5 Script Outline format (one subsection per script)

**Filename:** [ebs_type_feature.js]

**Script ID:** [customscript_ebs_type_feature]

**Script Type:** [RESTlet / User Event / Map-Reduce / Suitelet / Scheduled]

**Script Parameters:** (omit this if there are none)

- [Label] (custscript_ebs_param_name) — [Type]: [Value or TBD]

**Script Outline:**

- **functionName(params)** — One-sentence description of what this function does

- **functionName(params)** — One-sentence description

## Rules

- No code or code snippets of any kind. Function names plus plain-language descriptions only.

- Functional requirements must be in table format. No prose lists.

- FR tables: exactly two columns. Never add a Notes or Status column.

- Data tables: navy header row, white body rows. No alternating fills.

- Section 4.5: function names in bold. Never use backticks.

- Script names with pipe characters: use a dash in the doc ("EBS - Script Name") — pipes break table rendering.

- No emojis. No filler.

- File the output as [CLIENT]-[Feature] SDD v1.docx

- Shortest document a client can approve and a developer can build. Nothing else

  earns a place.

- No restating. If it is in the FR table, it is not repeated as prose in Section 4.

- Cut every "This section describes" and "In order to" opener.

- One idea per sentence.

- Current State and Desired State are bullets, not narrative. Five bullets each is

  usually enough.

- Technical Approach is what gets built. Justification is one sentence per script,

  no more.

- Assumptions are testable statements the client can agree or disagree with, not hedges.

- If a section has nothing real in it, write "Not applicable to this solution."

  Do not pad it.

- Banned words: robust, seamless, leverage, utilize, ensure, streamline,

  comprehensive, as needed, where appropriate.

